{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["After the firstborn and the Levites were counted, and the Levites were commanded concerning their service and their guard-duty, and the wagons were given to them, He completed the commandments of the priests concerning the Menorah. Now He comes to command the commandments of the Levites, that they should begin to serve, and it was necessary to purify them and to make atonement for them, and this is the reason this pericah is juxtaposed here. And He completed the commandment for all generations: <b>From twenty-five years old and upward they shall come to serve in the host</b> (Numbers 8:24). And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Numbers 8:24): Here it says \"from twenty-five\" and in another place (Numbers 4:3) it says \"from thirty years old.\" How so? From twenty-five he comes to learn the laws of the service and studies for five years, and at thirty he serves. From here [we learn] concerning a student who sits before his master and has not seen a favorable sign in his learning after five years — he will no longer see. And if so, the meaning of <b>they shall come to serve in the host</b> would be that he comes to assemble among the assemblies at the service of the Tent of Meeting, that he stands with them and constantly observes their service by day and by night, so that he learns the laws of the service through study and through practice. And therefore it does not say here \"to perform service\" as is said above, but rather it says <b>to serve in the host in the service of the Tent of Meeting</b> — that he comes together with those who serve in the service.\n\nBut I do not know whether this is the view of all our Rabbis, for in the language of an individual I repeat it in Sifrei (Beha'alotkha 63): Rabbi Nathan says, one verse says \"from twenty-five\" etc. And also it appears that they are words of asmakhta — that students were accustomed to study the laws of the service for five years.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, those counted by the hand of Moses and Aaron were from thirty years old and upward, and they are the ones whom he appointed, each man, over his service and over his burden. But here He commanded that anyone who knows of himself that he has reached the age of twenty-five shall be fit for service, and he may come of his own desire to serve with them and assist them in the service, but he shall not be an appointed officer over a specific service. And the reason for this is that every man is mindful of himself in his twenties, for his nature changes during them, and it becomes known when he reaches twenty or thirty — for his neighbors and relatives and his father and mother and his children know it and make it known to him — but when he reaches twenty-five they do not notice him as much. Therefore He did not command Moses to trouble himself to count except from thirty, at which age a man is recognized.\n\nAnd so David said: \"And the Levites were counted from thirty years old and upward\" (1 Chronicles 23:3).\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 8:24) according to the plain sense, that from thirty is for the service of burdens and from twenty-five is for the service of the Tent. But this is not correct, for there too it is also said: \"all who come to serve in the host, to perform service in the Tent of Meeting\" (Numbers 4:23), and it is said: \"to serve and to bear burdens\" (Numbers 4:24). And furthermore, it is written concerning the Temple: \"These were the sons of Levi according to their fathers' houses, heads of the fathers according to their count, by name, by their polling, doing the work for the service of the house of YHWH, from twenty years old and upward. For David said: YHWH, the Elohim of Israel, has given rest to His people, and He dwells in Jerusalem forever; and also the Levites have no need to carry the Tabernacle and all its vessels for its service. For by the last words of David these are the number of the sons of Levi from twenty years old and upward. For their station is by the hand of the sons of Aaron for the service of the house of YHWH, in the courts and in the chambers\" (1 Chronicles 23:24, 27–28) — he says that by the last words of David, once the House was built and they had no burden on the shoulder and their service was only in the courts and in the chambers, he counted them from twenty years old and upward, but at first, from thirty. And if according to Rabbi Abraham's words, he should have been required to count them from twenty-five."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:53.574472296Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}